<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Workingmen's Association</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as First International, International Working Men's Association</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Workingmen's Association, commonly known as the First International, was a political international that aimed to unite left-wing groups and trade unions of the working class. It was founded at a meeting of some 2,000 workers in St Martin's Hall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 28 September 1864, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> drafted its Inaugural Address and Provisional Rules. Conflict between Marx's supporters and the anarchists around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a> led to a split at the Hague Congress of 1872, which expelled Bakunin and moved the General Council to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The International was formally disbanded in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association.jpg" alt="International Workingmen's Association" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:International-founding-1864.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>International Workingmen’s Association</b>, the First International, was founded at St Martin’s Hall in London on 28 September 1864; divided between the followers of Marx and the anarchists around Mikhail Bakunin, it split in 1872 and was dissolved in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Mikhail Bakunin entered the <b>International Workingmen’s Association</b>, formed in 1864, where his faction and Proudhon’s mutualists opposed state socialism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In the last third of the 19th century democratic socialist parties, drawing mainly on Marxism, arose across Europe, while the split between authoritarian and libertarian socialists went back to the <b>First International</b> and its expulsion of the anarchists in 1872.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1934, at the request of the theatre director Erwin Piscator, Brecht wrote the song for the <b>First International</b> Workers' Music Olympiad, held in Strasbourg in 1935, where a choir of 3,000 workers gave its first performance.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li><li><blockquote>Eugène Pottier, a member of the <b>First International</b> who had attended its congress of 1864 and a member of the Paris Commune, wrote the French words in June 1871, after the French army crushed the Commune on 28 May and before he fled to Britain and then, from…</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li></ul></section>
